  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the following congruences have *precisely three* solutions modulo $24?$\n\nI. $18x≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24)$\n\nII. $9x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24)$\n\nIII. $15x≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24)$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14269988/assets/b65fc4830e0b060d62226b41d5ccb7bdc63d0ad594f7a42a2628597409bba3af.png)\n\n      \n\nRecall the following facts regarding the congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n):$\n\n- The congruence has no solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,b.\n$$\n\n- The congruence has $\\text{gcd}(a,n)$ solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,|\\,b.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our congruences.\n\n- Congruence I has six solutions modulo $24.$ We have $a=18$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(18,24)=6.\n$$\n\nSince $b=12$ is divisible by $6,$ this congruence has $6$ solutions.\n\n- Congruence II has no solutions. We have $a=9$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(9,24)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $b=5$ is not divisible by $3,$ this congruence has no solutions.\n\n- Congruence III has three solutions modulo $24.$ We have $a=15$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(15,24)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $b=18$ is divisible by $3,$ this congruence has $3$ solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
